moment (ignoring location and monotonic differences); ",[18,1559,734],{}," compares the full struct (location, monotonic), so two representations of the same instant can be ",[18,1562,1563],{},"!=",". This is the #1 time bug.",[1526,1566,1567,1569,1570,990,1572,1575,1576,1579,1580,1583],{},[223,1568,1530],{},": use IANA time zone names (",[18,1571,857],{},[18,1573,1574],{},"\"EST\"",") — IANA names have full DST rules; ",[18,1577,1578],{},"EST"," is a fixed offset (no DST). ",[18,1581,1582],{},"time.LoadLocation(\"America\u002FNew_York\")"," is the right way.",[1526,1585,1586,1588,1589,1592,1593,1595,1596,1599],{},[223,1587,1530],{},": format dates with the reference time (",[18,1590,1591],{},"t.Format(\"2006-01-02\")","), not ",[18,1594,229],{}," — Go's formatting is by example, where ",[18,1597,1598],{},"2006-01-02 15:04:05"," is the reference (1\u002F2 3:04:05PM '06). It's unusual but unambiguous and self-documenting.",[1526,1601,1602,1531,1604,1607,1608,1611,1612,1614,1615,1617,1618,1621,1622,1624,1625,1627],{},[223,1603,1530],{},[18,1605,1606],{},"time.NewTicker"," with ",[18,1609,1610],{},"defer ticker.Stop()"," for periodic work, and include ",[18,1613,1513],{}," in the ",[18,1616,1295],{}," so the tick loop can be canceled — a ticker without an exit path leaks the goroutine. ",[18,1619,1620],{},"time.After"," leaks the timer if another case wins; use ",[18,1623,1270],{}," + ",[18,1626,1178],{}," in hot loops.",[39,1629,1631],{"id":1630},"️-edge-cases-gotchas","⚠️ Edge Cases & Gotchas",[1523,1633,1634,1651,1670,1683,1694,1712,1729,1744,1763,1780],{},[1526,1635,1636,1642,1643,1646,1647,1650],{},[223,1637,1638,606,1640],{},[18,1639,734],{},[18,1641,737],{},": ",[18,1644,1645],{},"t1 == t2"," is false for the same instant in different locations. Use ",[18,1648,1649],{},"t1.Equal(t2)",".",[1526,1652,1653,1642,1658,33,1660,1662,1663,1666,1667,1669],{},[223,1654,1655,1656],{},"Monotonic clock only in ",[18,1657,1109],{},[18,1659,1099],{},[18,1661,1102],{}," have no monotonic reading — ",[18,1664,1665],{},"time.Since(parsedTime)"," uses the wall clock (subject to jumps). Use ",[18,1668,1109],{}," for start times of elapsed measurements.",[1526,1671,1672,1677,1678,1624,1681,1650],{},[223,1673,1674,1676],{},[18,1675,1620],{}," leaks",": the timer goroutine lingers until the duration, even if the select took another case. In hot loops, use ",[18,1679,1680],{},"time.NewTimer",[18,1682,1178],{},[1526,1684,1685,1642,1690,1693],{},[223,1686,1687,1689],{},[18,1688,487],{}," normalizes",[18,1691,1692],{},"time.Date(2024, 1, 31).AddDate(0, 1, 0)"," = Feb 29 (2024 is a leap year) or Feb 28 — \"Jan 31 + 1 month\" normalizes to the last day of Feb.",[1526,1695,1696,1642,1701,1704,1705,1708,1709,1711],{},[223,1697,1698,1700],{},[18,1699,1102],{}," uses UTC if no zone",[18,1702,1703],{},"time.Parse(time.RFC3339, \"2024-03-15T14:30:00\")"," (no ",[18,1706,1707],{},"Z"," or offset) parses as UTC. Use ",[18,1710,202],{}," for a specific zone.",[1526,1713,1714,1642,1719,1721,1722,1725,1726,1711],{},[223,1715,1716,1718],{},[18,1717,1109],{}," is local",[18,1720,1109],{}," returns local time. Use ",[18,1723,1724],{},".UTC()"," for UTC display, ",[18,1727,1728],{},".In(loc)",[1526,1730,1731,1642,1736,1739,1740,1743],{},[223,1732,1733,1735],{},[18,1734,1506],{}," isn't cancellable",[18,1737,1738],{},"time.Sleep(10*time.Second)"," blocks for 10s regardless. Use ",[18,1741,1742],{},"select { case \u003C-time.After(10s): case \u003C-ctx.Done(): }"," for cancellable waits.",[1526,1745,1746,1642,1751,1754,1755,1758,1759,1762],{},[223,1747,1748,1749],{},"DST and ",[18,1750,464],{},[18,1752,1753],{},"t.Add(24 * time.Hour)"," adds 24 hours of ",[744,1756,1757],{},"elapsed time",", not \"the same time tomorrow\" — across a DST spring-forward, 24h from 1 AM is 2 AM (not 1 AM) the next day. Use ",[18,1760,1761],{},"AddDate(0, 0, 1)"," for \"same wall time tomorrow.\"",[1526,1764,1765,1771,1772,1775,1776,1779],{},[223,1766,1767,1770],{},[18,1768,1769],{},"time.LoadLocation"," on Windows",": Windows doesn't have ",[18,1773,1774],{},"\u002Fusr\u002Fshare\u002Fzoneinfo","; import ",[18,1777,1778],{},"_ \"time\u002Ftzdata\""," to embed the tz database in your binary.",[1526,1781,1782,1642,1787,1790,1791,1794,1795,1650],{},[223,1783,1784,1786],{},[18,1785,44],{}," zero value",[18,1788,1789],{},"time.Time{}"," is year 1, January 1, 00:00:00 UTC — a valid but \"zero\" time. Use ",[18,1792,1793],{},"t.IsZero()"," to check. JSON marshals it as ",[18,1796,1797],{},"\"0001-01-01T00:00:00Z\"",[39,1799,1801],{"id":1800},"spot-the-bug","🧠 Spot the Bug",[14,1803,1804],{},"A developer measures elapsed time but gets wrong results after the server's clock is adjusted by NTP:",[47,1806,1807,1948,1954,1973,1977],{"language":49},[51,1808,1810],{"className":53,"code":1809,"language":49,"meta":55,"style":55},"start := time.Date(2024, 3, 15, 14, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC)\n\u002F\u002F ... work ...\nelapsed := time.Since(start)\n``\n::\n\nWhat's wrong?\n\n\u003Cdetails>\n\u003Csummary>Answer\u003C\u002Fsummary>\n\n`time.Date(...)` creates a `Time` with a wall-clock reading but **no monotonic clock component** (only `time.Now()` includes a monotonic reading). `time.Since(start)` computes `time.Now().Sub(start)` — since `start` has no monotonic reading, the subtraction uses the wall clock, which is subject to NTP adjustments. If the clock jumped backward during the work, `elapsed` could be negative or wrong.\n\nThe fix — use `time.Now()` for the start time (it has a monotonic reading), so `time.Since` uses the monotonic clock (immune to wall-clock changes):\n\n```go\nstart := time.Now()   \u002F\u002F ✅ includes monotonic reading\n\u002F\u002F ... work ...\nelapsed := time.Since(start)   \u002F\u002F uses monotonic clock — accurate even if wall clock jumps\n",[18,1811,1812,1853,1857,1872,1876,1880,1884,1894,1898,1903,1908,1912,1917,1921,1926,1930,1934,1939,1943],{"__ignoreMap":55},[59,1813,1814,1817,1819,1821,1823,1825,1827,1829,1831,1833,1835,1837,1839,1841,1843,1845,1847,1849,1851],{"class":61,"line":62},[59,1815,1816],{"class":65},"start ",[59,1818,70],{"class":69},[59,1820,73],{"class":65},[59,1822,97],{"class":76},[59,1824,100],{"class":65},[59,1826,104],{"class":103},[59,1828,107],{"class":65},[59,1830,110],{"class":103},[59,1832,107],{"class":65},[59,1834,115],{"class":103},[59,1836,107],{"class":65},[59,1838,120],{"class":103},[59,1840,107],{"class":65},[59,1842,130],{"class":103},[59,1844,107],{"class":65},[59,1846,130],{"class":103},[59,1848,107],{"class":65},[59,1850,130],{"class":103},[59,1852,213],{"class":65},[59,1854,1855],{"class":61,"line":87},[59,1856,1077],{"class":83},[59,1858,1859,1862,1864,1866,1869],{"class":61,"line":143},[59,1860,1861],{"class":65},"elapsed ",[59,1863,70],{"class":69},[59,1865,73],{"class":65},[59,1867,1868],{"class":76},"Since",[59,1870,1871],{"class":65},"(start)\n",[59,1873,1874],{"class":61,"line":173},[59,1875,532],{"class":158},[59,1877,1878],{"class":61,"line":193},[59,1879,538],{"class":65},[59,1881,1882],{"class":61,"line":417},[59,1883,400],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":399},[59,1885,1886,1889,1891],{"class":61,"line":428},[59,1887,1888],{"class":65},"What",[59,1890,1449],{"class":158},[59,1892,1893],{"class":1452},"s wrong?\n",[59,1895,1896],{"class":61,"line":442},[59,1897,400],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":399},[59,1899,1900],{"class":61,"line":447},[59,1901,1902],{"class":1452},"\u003Cdetails>\n",[59,1904,1905],{"class":61,"line":453},[59,1906,1907],{"class":1452},"\u003Csummary>Answer\u003C\u002Fsummary>\n",[59,1909,1910],{"class":61,"line":477},[59,1911,400],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":399},[59,1913,1914],{"class":61,"line":509},[59,1915,1916],{"class":1452},"`time.Date(...)` creates a `Time` with a wall-clock reading but **no monotonic clock component** (only `time.Now()` includes a monotonic reading). `time.Since(start)` computes `time.Now().Sub(start)` — since `start` has no monotonic reading, the subtraction uses the wall clock, which is subject to NTP adjustments. If the clock jumped backward during the work, `elapsed` could be negative or wrong.\n",[59,1918,1919],{"class":61,"line":529},[59,1920,400],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":399},[59,1922,1923],{"class":61,"line":535},[59,1924,1925],{"class":1452},"The fix — use `time.Now()` for the start time (it has a monotonic reading), so `time.Since` uses the monotonic clock (immune to wall-clock changes):\n",[59,1927,1928],{"class":61,"line":541},[59,1929,400],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":399},[59,1931,1932],{"class":61,"line":546},[59,1933,705],{"class":1452},[59,1935,1936],{"class":61,"line":592},[59,1937,1938],{"class":1452},"start := time.Now()   \u002F\u002F ✅ includes monotonic reading\n",[59,1940,1941],{"class":61,"line":597},[59,1942,1077],{"class":1452},[59,1944,1945],{"class":61,"line":612},[59,1946,1947],{"class":1452},"elapsed := time.Since(start)   \u002F\u002F uses monotonic clock — accurate even if wall clock jumps\n",[14,1949,1950,1951,1953],{},"If you need a specific start time (e.g., from a parsed timestamp), you can't get monotonic accuracy — monotonic clocks can't be reconstructed from wall times. Measure elapsed time from ",[18,1952,1109],{}," at the actual start.",[14,1955,1956,1642,1959,1962,1963,1965,1966,33,1968,1970,1971,1650],{},[223,1957,1958],{},"The lesson",[18,1960,1961],{},"time.Since"," uses the monotonic clock only if both times have a monotonic reading. ",[18,1964,1109],{}," has one; ",[18,1967,1099],{},[18,1969,1102],{}," don't. For elapsed-time measurement, always start with ",[18,1972,1109],{},[39,1974,1976],{"id":1975},"summary","Summary",[14,1978,1979,1980,1982,1983,1985,1986,1988,1989,1991,1992,1624,1994,1996,1997,33,1999,33,2001,2003],{},"You can now use ",[18,1981,44],{}," (instants), ",[18,1984,342],{}," (spans), format with the reference time, compare with ",[18,1987,737],{}," (not ",[18,1990,734],{},"), handle time zones with IANA names, measure elapsed time with the monotonic clock (",[18,1993,1109],{},[18,1995,1961],{},"), and use ",[18,1998,32],{},[18,2000,36],{},[18,2002,1502],{}," with cancellation. Next: testing and benchmarking.",[2005,2006,2007],"style",{},"html pre.shiki code .ssxIu, html code.shiki .ssxIu{--shiki-default:#24292E;--shiki-github-dark:#E1E4E8}html pre.shiki code .svdQ7, html code.shiki .svdQ7{--shiki-default:#D73A49;--shiki-github-dark:#F97583}html pre.shiki code .sIsaT, html code.shiki .sIsaT{--shiki-default:#6F42C1;--shiki-github-dark:#B392F0}html pre.shiki code .sdCPZ, html code.shiki .sdCPZ{--shiki-default:#6A737D;--shiki-github-dark:#6A737D}html pre.shiki code .snvgF, html code.shiki .snvgF{--shiki-default:#005CC5;--shiki-github-dark:#79B8FF}html pre.shiki code .sJ6F3, html code.shiki .sJ6F3{--shiki-default:#032F62;--shiki-github-dark:#9ECBFF}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html.github-dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-github-dark);background: var(--shiki-github-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-github-dark-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-github-dark-text-decoration);}html pre.shiki code .sMKGj, html code.shiki .sMKGj{--shiki-default:#B31D28;--shiki-default-font-style:italic;--shiki-github-dark:#FDAEB7;--shiki-github-dark-font-style:italic}",{"title":55,"searchDepth":87,"depth":87,"links":2009},[2010,2014,2019,2023,2025,2026,2027,2028],{"id":41,"depth":87,"text":2011,"children":2012},"time.Time — an Instant",[2013],{"id":217,"depth":143,"text":218},{"id":339,"depth":87,"text":2015,"children":2016},"time.Duration — a Span",[2017],{"id":731,"depth":143,"text":2018},"== vs Equal",{"id":832,"depth":87,"text":833,"children":2020},[2021],{"id":1106,"depth":143,"text":2022},"time.Now() and serialization",{"id":1128,"depth":87,"text":2024},"time.Ticker and time.Timer",{"id":1520,"depth":87,"text":1521},{"id":1630,"depth":87,"text":1631},{"id":1800,"depth":87,"text":1801},{"id":1975,"depth":87,"text":1976},"Go's time package handles instants (Time), durations (Duration), and ticking (Ticker\u002FTimer). It's well-designed but has time-zone and monotonic-clock subtleties.","md",{},"\u002Fgo\u002F24-time-and-dates",{"title":5,"description":2029},"go\u002F24-time-and-dates","uO6vHwvHpJJi1NBahW1i6FHDeEIsrf4HLj2lqZTSQO4",1786908610230]